> Here’s a practical briefing for the visit. I’d treat the student/organizer profiles as somewhat tentative unless confirmed on the department site, but the broad conversation angles should help.
> 
> | Person | What to know / likely angle |
> |---|---|
> | **Jeffrey Sun** | Visit organizer; likely your logistics anchor. Thank him early, confirm timing/rooms, and ask who in the department is most interested in climate impacts, adaptation, and empirical macro-environment links. |
> | **Jacquelyn Pless — Rotman School of Management** | Energy and environmental innovation scholar. Strong overlap with climate tech, clean-energy policy, R&D, firm behavior, and how policy design affects innovation. Good topics: evidence on induced innovation, adaptation technology, translating climate-damage estimates into investment/policy decisions. |
> | **Christian Spielmann** | UofT economics meeting; I’d prepare for applied micro/econometrics or environmental/development-adjacent discussion, but verify his exact field. Ask about local seminar culture and how the department thinks about empirical climate-economy work. |
> | **Karen Clay** | Major economic historian/environmental economist, known for work on pollution, energy, health, regulation, and long-run U.S. environmental history. Excellent person for historical analogues to climate damages, spatial inequality, adaptation, and credibility of empirical damage estimates. |
> | **Larasati Wulandari** | Lunch organizer and later meeting; likely graduate student or junior researcher. Good chance to ask about the student perspective, department climate, seminar norms, and who is excited by climate/development/environment work. |
> | **Benjamin Lauzon** | Lunch participant; likely graduate student. Use lunch to learn what students are working on and how your seminar lands with empirically minded audiences. |
> | **Ayaka Behro** | Likely graduate student/junior researcher. Good topics: her research interests, data/methods overlap, and whether students see opportunities in climate-impact meta-analysis or integrated assessment work. |
> | **Eduardo Souza-Rodrigues** | Environmental/resource economist at UofT. Works on land use, deforestation, development/environment tradeoffs, and structural/dynamic empirical models. Strong overlap with your interests in spatial climate impacts, policy counterfactuals, and rigorous damage estimation. |
> | **Hugo Cordeau** | Likely graduate student/junior researcher. Ask about research fit, departmental strengths, and methodological preferences in seminars. |
> | **Christoph Semken** | UofT economist; likely relevant to environmental, urban/spatial, trade, or applied micro themes. Since he is also at dinner, save broader, more speculative conversation for then: climate damages, distributional impacts, and how economists should communicate uncertainty. |
> 
> **Seminar framing tip:** for “Economic disparities in a warming world,” expect interest in identification, publication bias/heterogeneity in the meta-analysis, external validity across income/temperature ranges, adaptation, and whether GDP impacts mask sectoral/distributional welfare effects. A strong line is: *the contribution is not just an average damage function, but a disciplined map of who is hurt, where estimates are robust, and where the literature is still thin.*
